I apologize for the delay in this posting, as I am writing this
Saturday, but won't be connected to the net until late Sunday/early
Monday.

They can be expert in darts and daggers, which provide a good ranged
defense.  I prefer to skip darts, and get daggers up to expert in the
beginning game, for multiple daggers/throw, just because daggers don't
have a chance of being destroyed in throwing.

Then, I tend to head to the mines and harvest myself a dwarvish mattock, 
a good twohanded weapon with significant damage.  (Dwarves are also a
good source of armour for combat wombats - dwarvish mithril, hats, and 
iron shoes!)  If you run the numbers, and consider the possibilities, 
there is a strong argument for mattocks as doing the most damage in the 
early game.

Tourists can also become skilled in unihorns, which do good damage, but
by the time you start seeing unicorns, you've probably had the
oppertunity to get the silver saber on the watch captain.  Since
tourists can be skilled in sabers, its probably worth training that
skill:  Sabers do not rust, do double damage against
demons/werecreatures, and Grayswandir is a hell of a weapon in the late
game.  Tourists can be skilled in #twoweaponing.  Grayswandir/silver
saber combo is pretty powerful - the only downside is that neutral
tourists don't normally get grayswandir by saccing.  Otherwise, 
Mjollnir and Frostband are the typical AK choices, but the to-hit chance 
on both of those will go down.  If your tourists tend to use ranged
attacks, look into Magicbane - its a good weapon to weild even if it
doesn't do much damage (then again, I tend to hit level 30, which makes
cursing a larger problem for me then most people).

Also, remember to get poison resistance ASAP!  Read the spoilers and
google if you need to figure out how to get it in the early game.
Tourists have poor strength - the drop due to poisoning tends to end up
killing them in the not-so-long run.  Exercize that strength as well -
as with all low level characters, consider visiting sokobon before
heading to the bottom of the mines.

I find that my tourists last longer when I carry methods of escape,
keep a few pets, and practice my calligraphy on the floor alot
(Elbereth, anyone?).  Learn to RUN!  RUN!  RUN!  Avoid being burdened
like the plague - it will kill you.

